                <pb facs="0840" n="1798" sameAs="frus:frus1888p2/pg1798"/>

                <head>No. 30.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Sir L. S. Sackville
                        West</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-10-22"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 22,
                        1887</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I had the honor of receiving last evening
                    your note of the 19th instant, conveying the instruction to you by the Marquis
                    of Salisbury that you should protest against the seizure of the Canadian vessel
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Alfred Adams</hi> in Behring Sea, and against the
                    continuance of similar proceedings by the United States authorities on the high
                    seas; and I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
